{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":"2026-02-24T16:49:38.319Z","tool_name":"write_to_file","tool_id":"tool-5","parameters":{"file_path":"/home/nicj/code/github.com/nicholasjackson/demo-sdd-terraform-opa/examples/docker/.sdd/plans/1_no_port_22_plan.md","content":"# Implementation Plan: Docker Container Security Policy\n\n## Overview\nThis plan outlines the implementation of an OPA (Open Policy Agent) Rego policy to enforce security best practices for Docker containers deployed via Terraform. The policy will validate Terraform plans before they are applied to ensure containers meet security requirements.\n\n## Specification Reference\n- **Spec File**: `./examples/docker/.sdd/specs/1_no_port_22`\n- **Target Terraform Code**: `./examples/docker/terraform/`\n\n## Requirements Summary\n\nThe policy must enforce the following security controls:\n\n1. **Port 22 Restriction**: No SSH port exposure\n2. **Privileged Mode**: Containers must not run in privileged mode\n3. **Read-only Filesystem**: Containers must have read-only root filesystems\n4. **Capability Restrictions**: Containers must drop all capabilities and only add required ones\n5. **Image Namespace**: Only images from the \"hashicorp\" namespace with explicit tags\n\n## Technical Architecture\n\n### File Structure\n```\nexamples/docker/.sdd/policies/\n├── docker_security.rego          # Main policy implementation\n├── docker_security_test.rego     # Comprehensive test suite\n└── test_fixtures/                # Test data directory\n    ├── valid_plan.json           # Compliant Terraform plan\n    ├── invalid_port_22.json      # Plan with SSH port exposed\n    ├── invalid_privileged.json   # Plan with privileged mode\n    ├── invalid_writable.json     # Plan with writable filesystem\n    ├── invalid_caps.json         # Plan without capability drops\n    └── invalid_image.json        # Plan with non-hashicorp image\n```\n\n### Package Structure\n```rego\npackage terraform.docker.security\n\nimport rego.v1\n```\n\n### Input Normalization\nThe policy must handle both raw Terraform and HCP Terraform/Enterprise input structures:\n\n```rego\n# Normalize input to work with both raw Terraform and HCP Terraform\ntfplan := object.get(input, \"plan\", input)\n```\n\n## Implementation Approach (Test-Driven Development)\n\n### Phase 1: Test Suite Creation\n**Before writing any policy code**, create `docker_security_test.rego` with comprehensive test cases.\n\n#### Test Categories\n\n1. **Port 22 Tests**\n   - `test_deny_port_22_standard_mapping` - Port 22 → 22\n   - `test_deny_port_22_non_standard_mapping` - Port 22 → 2222\n   - `test_allow_non_ssh_ports` - Port 8200 only\n   - `test_allow_no_port_mappings` - No ports defined\n\n2. **Privileged Mode Tests**\n   - `test_deny_privileged_mode_true` - Privileged = true\n   - `test_allow_privileged_mode_false` - Privileged = false\n   - `test_allow_privileged_mode_unset` - Privileged not defined\n\n3. **Read-only Filesystem Tests**\n   - `test_deny_writable_filesystem` - ReadonlyRootfs = false or unset\n   - `test_allow_readonly_filesystem` - ReadonlyRootfs = true\n\n4. **Capability Tests**\n   - `test_deny_no_cap_drop` - CapDrop not defined\n   - `test_deny_partial_cap_drop` - CapDrop doesn't include \"ALL\"\n   - `test_allow_drop_all_add_specific` - CapDrop = [\"ALL\"], CapAdd = [\"IPC_LOCK\"]\n   - `test_allow_drop_all_no_add` - CapDrop = [\"ALL\"], no CapAdd\n\n5. **Image Namespace Tests**\n   - `test_deny_unknown_namespace` - Image: nginx:latest\n   - `test_deny_hashicorp_no_tag` - Image: hashicorp/vault\n   - `test_deny_hashicorp_latest_tag` - Image: hashicorp/vault:latest\n   - `test_allow_hashicorp_explicit_tag` - Image: hashicorp/vault:1.15\n\n#### Test Data Structure\nEach test will use the `with` keyword to mock Terraform plan JSON:\n\n```rego\ntest_deny_port_22_standard_mapping if {\n    deny contains \"Port 22 (SSH) must not be exposed\" with input as {\n        \"resource_changes\": [{\n            \"type\": \"docker_container\",\n            \"change\": {\n                \"actions\": [\"create\"],\n                \"after\": {\n                    \"ports\": [{\n                        \"internal\": 22,\n                        \"external\": 22\n                    }]\n                }\n            }\n        }]\n    }\n}\n```\n\n### Phase 2: Policy Implementation\n\nAfter tests are written and confirmed to fail, implement the policy rules.\n\n#### Rule 1: Port 22 Validation\n```rego\ndeny contains msg if {\n    some r in tfplan.resource_changes\n    r.type == \"docker_container\"\n    some action in r.change.actions; action in {\"create\", \"update\"}\n    \n    some port in r.change.after.ports\n    port.internal == 22\n    \n    msg := sprintf(\n        \"Container %v: Port 22 (SSH) must not be exposed (mapped to host port %v)\",\n        [r.address, port.external]\n    )\n}\n```\n\n#### Rule 2: Privileged Mode Validation\n```rego\ndeny contains msg if {\n    some r in tfplan.resource_changes\n    r.type == \"docker_container\"\n    some action in r.change.actions; action in {\"create\", \"update\"}\n    \n    r.change.after.privileged == true\n    \n    msg := sprintf(\n        \"Container %v: Privileged mode is not allowed\",\n        [r.address]\n    )\n}\n```\n\n#### Rule 3: Read-only Filesystem Validation\n```rego\ndeny contains msg if {\n    some r in tfplan.resource_changes\n    r.type == \"docker_container\"\n    some action in r.change.actions; action in {\"create\", \"update\"}\n    \n    not r.change.after.read_only\n    \n    msg := sprintf(\n        \"Container %v: Root filesystem must be read-only\",\n        [r.address]\n    )\n}\n```\n\n#### Rule 4: Capability Validation\n```rego\ndeny contains msg if {\n    some r in tfplan.resource_changes\n    r.type == \"docker_container\"\n    some action in r.change.actions; action in {\"create\", \"update\"}\n    \n    not capabilities_properly_configured(r.change.after)\n    \n    msg := sprintf(\n        \"Container %v: Must drop ALL capabilities (--cap-drop=ALL) before adding specific ones\",\n        [r.address]\n    )\n}\n\ncapabilities_properly_configured(container) if {\n    some cap in container.capabilities.drop\n    upper(cap) == \"ALL\"\n}\n```\n\n#### Rule 5: Image Namespace Validation\n```rego\ndeny contains msg if {\n    some r in tfplan.resource_changes\n    r.type == \"docker_container\"\n    some action in r.change.actions; action in {\"create\", \"update\"}\n    \n    not valid_image(r.change.after.image)\n    \n    msg := sprintf(\n        \"Container %v: Image must be from hashicorp namespace with explicit version tag (got: %v)\",\n        [r.address, r.change.after.image]\n    )\n}\n\nvalid_image(image) if {\n    startswith(image, \"hashicorp/\")\n    contains(image, \":\")\n    not endswith(image, \":latest\")\n}\n```\n\n### Phase 3: Terraform Plan JSON Generation\n\nGenerate a Terraform plan in JSON format for testing:\n\n```bash\ncd examples/docker/terraform\nterraform init\nterraform plan -out=tfplan\nterraform show -json tfplan > tfplan.json\n```\n\n### Phase 4: Policy Testing\n\nRun OPA tests to validate policy implementation:\n\n```bash\ncd examples/docker/.sdd/policies\nopa test . -v\n```\n\nExpected output:\n```\ndocker_security_test.rego:\n  PASS: test_deny_port_22_standard_mapping (0.5ms)\n  PASS: test_deny_port_22_non_standard_mapping (0.4ms)\n  PASS: test_allow_non_ssh_ports (0.3ms)\n  ...\n  PASS: 20/20\n```\n\n### Phase 5: Integration Testing\n\nTest the policy against the actual Terraform plan:\n\n```bash\nopa eval \\\n  --data examples/docker/.sdd/policies/docker_security.rego \\\n  --input examples/docker/terraform/tfplan.json \\\n  --format pretty \\\n  \"data.terraform.docker.security.deny\"\n```\n\n## Current Terraform Code Analysis\n\n### Existing Configuration (`main.tf`)\nThe current Terraform configuration creates a Vault container with:\n\n- ✅ **Image**: `hashicorp/vault:${var.vault_version}` (default: 1.15) - COMPLIANT\n- ✅ **Port**: 8200 (not SSH) - COMPLIANT\n- ❌ **Privileged**: Not set (defaults to false) - NEEDS EXPLICIT SETTING\n- ❌ **Read-only**: Not set - NON-COMPLIANT\n- ❌ **Capabilities**: Only adds IPC_LOCK, doesn't drop ALL first - NON-COMPLIANT\n\n### Required Terraform Changes\n\nTo make the existing configuration compliant, the following changes are needed:\n\n```hcl\nresource \"docker_container\" \"vault\" {\n  # ... existing configuration ...\n  \n  # Add explicit privileged setting\n  privileged = false\n  \n  # Add read-only root filesystem\n  read_only = true\n  \n  # Fix capabilities - drop ALL first, then add required ones\n  capabilities {\n    drop = [\"ALL\"]\n    add  = [\"IPC_LOCK\"]\n  }\n  \n  # ... rest of configuration ...\n}\n```\n\n## Validation Strategy\n\n### Automated Testing\n1. **Unit Tests**: All `_test.rego` tests must pass\n2. **Integration Tests**: Policy must correctly evaluate actual Terraform plans\n3. **Coverage**: Use `opa test --coverage` to ensure >90% coverage\n\n### Manual Validation\n1. Generate compliant and non-compliant Terraform plans\n2. Verify policy correctly identifies violations\n3. Confirm error messages are clear and actionable\n\n## Success Criteria\n\nThe implementation is complete when:\n\n1. ✅ All unit tests in `docker_security_test.rego` pass\n2. ✅ Policy correctly validates all 5 security requirements\n3. ✅ Policy works with both raw Terraform and HCP Terraform input formats\n4. ✅ Error messages clearly identify the violation and affected resource\n5. ✅ Policy follows Rego best practices (uses `import rego.v1`, proper naming, etc.)\n6. ✅ Test coverage is >90%\n7. ✅ Policy can be integrated into CI/CD pipeline\n\n## Implementation Timeline\n\n1. **Day 1**: Create test suite (`docker_security_test.rego`)\n2. **Day 1**: Implement policy rules (`docker_security.rego`)\n3. **Day 1**: Run unit tests and iterate until all pass\n4. **Day 2**: Generate Terraform plan JSON and test integration\n5. **Day 2**: Document policy usage and integration steps\n6. **Day 2**: Create example CI/CD pipeline configuration\n\n## Integration with CI/CD\n\n### GitHub Actions Example\n```yaml\nname: Terraform Policy Validation\n\non: [pull_request]\n\njobs:\n  opa-validate:\n    runs-on: ubuntu-latest\n    steps:\n      - uses: actions/checkout@v3\n      \n      - name: Setup OPA\n        uses: open-policy-agent/setup-opa@v2\n        with:\n          version: latest\n      \n      - name: Setup Terraform\n        uses: hashicorp/setup-terraform@v2\n      \n      - name: Terraform Plan\n        run: |\n          cd examples/docker/terraform\n          terraform init\n          terraform plan -out=tfplan\n          terraform show -json tfplan > tfplan.json\n      \n      - name: Run OPA Policy\n        run: |\n          opa eval \\\n            --data examples/docker/.sdd/policies/docker_security.rego \\\n            --input examples/docker/terraform/tfplan.json \\\n            --format pretty \\\n            --fail-defined \\\n            \"data.terraform.docker.security.deny\"\n```\n\n## Documentation Deliverables\n\n1. **Policy Documentation**: Explain each rule and its rationale\n2. **Usage Guide**: How to run the policy locally and in CI/CD\n3. **Troubleshooting Guide**: Common issues and solutions\n4. **Compliance Report**: Template for documenting policy compliance\n\n## Risk Mitigation\n\n### Potential Issues\n1. **Terraform Provider Schema Changes**: Docker provider schema may change\n   - **Mitigation**: Use Terraform MCP server to verify current schema\n   \n2. **False Positives**: Policy may incorrectly flag compliant resources\n   - **Mitigation**: Comprehensive test suite with edge cases\n   \n3. **Performance**: Policy evaluation may be slow for large plans\n   - **Mitigation**: Use efficient Rego patterns, avoid unnecessary iterations\n\n## Next Steps\n\n1. Create the policy directory structure\n2. Implement the test suite following TDD principles\n3. Implement the policy rules\n4. Validate against actual Terraform plans\n5. Document the policy and integration process\n6. Create CI/CD pipeline examples\n\n## References\n\n- OPA Documentation: https://www.openpolicyagent.org/docs/latest/\n- Rego Language Guide: https://www.openpolicyagent.org/docs/latest/policy-language/\n- Terraform Docker Provider: https://registry.terraform.io/providers/kreuzwerker/docker/latest/docs\n- Docker Security Best Practices: https://docs.docker.com/engine/security/\n"}}
